lesson.body:
When you are designing an algorithm to solve a new problem, the skill from Algorithm Design, the very first working solution you write is often a brute force approach: try every possibility, check each one against what the problem requires, and return the ones that qualify. Brute force is rarely the fastest approach, but it is usually the easiest to get correct, which makes it an excellent starting point.

Consider the problem of finding two numbers in a list that add up to a target value. A brute force solution checks every possible pair.

def two_sum_brute_force(numbers, target):
    for i in range(len(numbers)):
        for j in range(i + 1, len(numbers)):
            if numbers[i] + numbers[j] == target:
                return (i, j)
    return None

This is a direct translation of the problem statement into code: find two numbers that sum to target becomes check every pair of numbers, and see if any pair sums to target. There is no cleverness here, no assumption about sorted input, no shortcut, just an exhaustive check of every possibility. That directness is brute force's main appeal: it is easy to convince yourself it is correct, because it literally checks everything the problem could possibly mean.

The cost of that simplicity, as the nested loop structure suggests, is time complexity. Checking every pair of n numbers is the same pattern from Quadratic Time, O(n^2). A smarter approach using a dictionary for O(1) lookups, the technique from Constant Time, can solve the same problem in O(n) time by remembering numbers you have already seen and checking, for each new number, whether target minus that number has appeared before. Brute force will always be correct, but it often leaves easy performance gains on the table.

Brute force is not something to avoid entirely. It plays several legitimate roles in real programming. It is often the fastest approach to write and verify, which matters when a problem is small, only run once, or urgent. It gives you a correct reference implementation to test faster algorithms against: if you write a clever O(n log n) solution later, you can compare its output to the brute force version on many random inputs to catch bugs. And for genuinely small inputs, the gap between O(n^2) and O(n) or O(n log n) may not matter in practice at all, since even a slow algorithm finishes quickly when n is small.

The habit worth building is this: write the brute force solution first when a problem is unfamiliar, confirm it is correct, and only then ask whether its time or space complexity is acceptable for the sizes of input you actually expect. If profiling or empirical analysis shows it is too slow, using the technique from Empirical Analysis, that is your cue to look for patterns, sorted data enabling binary search, a hash table enabling O(1) lookups, or divide and conquer enabling O(n log n), that can replace brute force's exhaustive search with something faster. Brute force is the baseline every faster algorithm in this unit is ultimately trying to beat.
